Phase Theory proposes that phase is the sole fundamental constituent of physical reality, with space, time, matter, light, geometry, and gravitation emerging from the global organization and consistency dynamics of a pre-geometric phase substrate. Within the existing formulation, gravity emerges primarily through spatial phase strain: gradients in phase stiffness modify the transport of coherent phase modes and topological defects, producing an effective refractive geometry that reproduces the gravitoelectric sector of general relativity in the appropriate weak-field limit.

This paper develops a proposed extension of that mechanism.

The central claim is that a complete Phase-Theoretic description of gravity cannot be sourced solely by static phase strain or phase density. A dynamical phase substrate must also possess phase currents: directed transport of phase organization through the update ordering. These currents are the Phase-Theoretic analog of mass-energy currents in general relativity and of electric currents in electromagnetism. Their macroscopic effect is proposed to generate a vector-like phase potential whose coupling to the emergent calibration of time produces off-diagonal temporal-spatial structure.

The resulting mechanism is termed the gravitomagnetic coupling of time.

