I. introduction

1.1 The missing dynamical half of emergent gravity

Phase Theory begins from a radical ontological simplification: physical reality is fundamentally a phase substrate whose global organization generates the structures ordinarily described as space, time, matter, light, fields, and geometry.

In the existing gravitational formulation, phase strain modifies the transport properties of the substrate. Spatially varying phase stiffness produces an effective refractive structure. Coherent modes and localized topological defects therefore follow trajectories determined by the spatial organization of the phase substrate.

In the weak-field limit, this mechanism produces an effective gravitational potential and recovers the familiar effects of gravitational physics, including gravitational redshift, light deflection, Shapiro delay, and geodesic motion.

However, a static phase-strain mechanism is incomplete.

A source can possess the same total phase-inconsistency energy and the same approximate static phase density while differing in its internal transport. A non-rotating body and a rotating body may have comparable mass-energy but radically different phase-current configurations.

